2026-11-22 16:58:34 - Agent_20261122165120 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 9 CodeQL results for `py/dump-depends-calls` in setuptools/depends.py. The results are largely benign intra-function calls (find_module, maybe_close, contextlib.closing, attribute accesses) flagged because they surround the marshal.load sink; submitting them individually as vulnerabilities would be noise. The genuinely relevant sink is `marshal.load(f)` at depends.py:133 (PY_COMPILED branch of get_module_constant) — unsafe deserialization (CWE-502) is theoretically possible if an attacker can plant a malicious .pyc on the dependency search path, but the target is a local file chosen by the packaging path/module name, so practical exploitability is low and requires prior file-write access; submitted as manual review (mr). Similarly `compile(f.read(), path, 'exec')` at line 137 is only a compile of source into a code object (no execution), so cannot alone execute code; submitted as mr for the same path-controlled input concern. The remaining sites (lines 113, 125, 130, 132, 133, 135, 140) — Attribute() reads, find_module, maybe_close, contextlib.closing, and get_frozen_object/get_module attribute lookups on trusted local files — were classified false positives. Also investigated bdist_egg.py scan_module's `marshal.load(f)` (line 383) discovered independently: it deserializes .pyc files inside the egg being built from the developer's own tree, only to enumerate symbol names, with no attacker-controlled remote input — false positive for a security vulnerability. Test files (test_depends.py, test_setuptools.py) referencing get_module_constant/Require are not exploitable code paths.
